  "articleBody": "Ever start a basement molding project only to find your trim isn't quite fitting? It's a super common issue, especially in basements where things aren't always perfectly square or level. The key is to take your time and not rush the process. First off, make sure your molding has had a few days to get used to the temperature and humidity of your basement before you even think about cutting it. This 'acclimation' helps prevent it from shrinking or expanding after it's installed. When you're cutting, measure each wall individually, even if they look the same. Then, *always* dry-fit the pieces before you nail them in. This lets you spot any little adjustments you need to make, like tiny miter cuts for those tricky corners. If you're seeing gaps, it might mean your nails are spaced too far apart. Adding another nail can often pull the molding tight against the wall. Sometimes, the problem is just that the floors are uneven. That's where a pro really comes in handy; they've got the tricks like scribing to make everything look seamless. Don't be afraid to call HH Remodeling LLC if you're pulling your hair out – we've seen it all and can help you get that perfect finish.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
